   The experimental results were obtained while adapting the above computing method in the Jacobian groups which heavily requires polynomial arithmetic. One can avoid polynomial arithmetic for the nodal curves with a smaller arithmetic genus. For example,  if $\deg f(x)=2$ or $3$, one can perform group operation by conducting only integer arithmetic \cite[Chapter 14]{CohFrey}. 


\begin{remark}

The work by D{\'e}ch{\`e}ne \cite{dechene2006} also suggests use of generalized Jacobian groups in cryptographic algorithms. Unlike our method, the suggested group operation is not based on Cantor’s method, and the security of suggested cryptographic algorithms is based on the hardness assumption of the discrete logarithm problem (DLP). On the other hand, the work \cite{galbraith} shows that the use of generalized Jacobian groups does not bring any advantage over Jacobian groups in respect to DLP. 
\end{remark}



